  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing NEON BLOCK**\n\nI\'m deep in an intensive analysis, meticulously inspecting the requirements for **NEON BLOCK**. I\'m pushing to design an exceptional game: deeply satisfying, fully tactile, offline, HTML5 Canvas puzzle! Reviewing every detail.\n\n\n**Defining Game Mechanics**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in nailing down the core mechanics of **NEON BLOCK**. I\'m leaning towards a 6x6 grid for readability and strategic depth. The hybrid mechanic, merging tile-merging and block-placement, is taking shape, and the tray concept with three active block pieces feels promising. I\'m focusing on making the game crystal-clear and rewarding.\n\n\n**Exploring Block Value System**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the block value system. I\'m considering using uniform values within each polyomino piece like 2, 4, 8, 16. Visual scanning is key, and this system looks promising! I am now considering the use of \"Prism/Wild\" or higher-tier pieces for later gameplay, as the max tile value grows. I am considering the use of 2x2 blocks or Z/T-tetromino shapes, as I refine the mechanics for a 6x6 grid.\n\n\n**Refining D-Pad Controls**\n\nI\'m currently designing intuitive D-pad and keyboard controls. The **Grid Cursor mode** is taking shape, with arrow keys for cursor movement and Enter/Space for placement. I\'m focusing on seamless tray piece selection, evaluating Tab, Q, E, 1-3, Shift, or cursor navigation. The question of rotatable versus fixed shapes is a key issue that I am considering, with potential impacts on the grid size.\n\n\n**Prioritizing D-Pad Flow**\n\nI\'ve made significant breakthroughs in D-pad control design. Keeping pieces fixed, like 1010! or Blockudoku, makes touch drag-and-drop and D-pad placement far more intuitive. I\'m focusing on a balanced piece bag of 1-4 cell shapes. I\'ve designed a unified focus navigation graph for D-pad/keyboard controls that covers all aspects, including Fire TV remotes, ensuring 100% control, focusing on Up/Down/Left/Right/Select/Back.\n\n\n**Optimizing UI Focus**\n\nI\'m now refining the user interface focus navigation. I\'ve designed a clear flow: moving from the bottom row of the 6x6 grid shifts focus into the tray pieces, with Left/Right selecting between them. Enter/Space selects and snaps focus back to the grid. Going Up from the top row accesses the action bar, and Down returns to the grid. Importantly, I\'ve standardized Enter and Space for placement/selection and included tray navigation.\n\n\n**Confirming Hybrid Mechanics**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into the interplay of tile-merging and block-placement. I\'m focusing on the core of **NEON BLOCK**: placing blocks, calculating immediate placement points, and the subsequent orthogonal cluster merges. The key is in that the placement of a block directly triggers the cluster merge, where connected, orthogonal clusters of 3+ (or 2+) tiles of the same value are merged. The immediate placement points are critical for rewarding the user.\n\n\n**Revising Merge Mechanics**\n\nI\'m now revising the merge mechanics for NEON BLOCK. Option A, the chain merge on placement, seems very promising for intuitive gameplay. I am weighing the effects of placement of a 2x2 square of 2s, and evaluating the merge rules. I\'m also considering Option B, a 2048-style merge, or even allowing for 1, 2, or 3-cell polyominoes.\n\n\n**Refining Merge Logic**\n\nI\'m now iterating on merge mechanics. The recent focus is on chain merges and orthogonal adjacency. I\'m leaning toward *Option C*: line clear and adjacency power merge. The core concept is **4+ connected matching tiles** merge. I\'m also exploring 3+ matching tiles with the idea of polyomino pieces having at most 2 matching cells in a row (or even mixed values).\n\n\n\n**Evaluating Merge Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now comparing merge options for **NEON BLOCK**. I\'m testing \"Triple-Link & Line-Burst\" versus other designs. Each tile on the 6x6 grid would have a Neon Power Value of 2, 4, 8... Blocks in the tray could be polyomino shapes, with alternating values like `[2, 4, 2]`. I\'m evaluating what happens when a block is placed, and how this impacts tile value propagation.\n\n\n**Defining Merge Power**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in nailing down the exact mechanics of merge power. I\'m focusing on a 3+ orthogonal, same-value tile merge into a single upgraded tile, with an automatic chain merge if the new tile triggers more matches! Full line/column clears remain as a powerful bonus. I\'m considering the tactical synergies.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Merge Sequencing**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on merge sequencing and its impact. Should merges or line clears happen first? I\'m leaning toward prioritizing merges. They keep upgraded high-value tiles, even if the line is also full, potentially avoiding accidental wipes or board clears. My focus is on rewarding player strategy.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Merge Strategy**\n\nI\'m now tracing the merge mechanics. I\'ve realized if a 6-cell line is full AND a 3+ cluster forms, merges happen first. This frees space, preventing a game over and building larger tiles. I\'m focusing on scenarios with a filled row like `[2, 2, 4, 8, 16, _]` to see the mechanics in action. I\'m also confirming what kind of block can have 2 matching cells.\n\n\n**Validating Tray Piece Values**\n\nI\'m now validating the tray piece values to ensure no self-merging and immediate rewards. The logic is now clear: `[2, 2, 4]` L-shape means two `2`s on the board merge to a `4`, plus an adjacent `4` in the piece to be placed. Testing this across various polyomino shapes feels great!\n\n\n**Defining Piece Placement**\n\nI\'m now focusing on piece placement and considering swiping or sliding. However, I\'ve concluded that the hybrid placement with 3+ connected merge is superior, ensuring seamless control across drag, tap, D-pad, and keyboard. Chain merges and line clears feel really satisfying.\n\n\n**Visualizing Gameplay Effects**\n\nI\'m now visualizing the gameplay effects! I\'m planning on adding peggios, \"+128 CHAIN \303\2273!\" popups, particle bursts, and subtle screen shake. Clearing rows or columns will trigger a plasma beam, awarding `sum(tiles) \303\227 2 \303\227 linesCleared`. I\'m now testing the design and behavior of a \"Smart Bag\", in the tray piece design.\n\n\n**Refining Aesthetic Design**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the design aesthetic. I\'m focusing on a dark background with vibrant blue, purple, and cyan glowing tiles, ensuring clean typography, subtle gradients, and polished transitions. I\'m fully committed to a landscape-first, responsive design for various Android tablet resolutions, desktop, and gracefully adapting to narrower viewports without clipping. I\'ve re-read the prompt and confirmed *no external dependencies*, including CDNs!\n\n\n**Evaluating Canvas Rendering**\n\nI\'m now determining how to structure the HTML5 Canvas and UI. I\'m focusing on rendering the entire interactive playfield within the Canvas, including the background grid, glowing neon tiles, and the tray pieces. I\'m considering using an overlay for the HUD elements and instructions.\n\n\n**Optimizing Canvas Rendering**\n\nI\'m now fully committed to optimizing the Canvas rendering. A crisp HiDPI HTML5 Canvas for the gameplay field, with buttery 60fps performance and glowing effects (shadowBlur, radial gradients, additive blending), is my key focus! I\'m planning to place zero layout jitter on all of the polyominoes! I want a clear ghost placement preview, valid-drop glow, and a drag-and-drop piece with cursor focus ring for Fire TV D-pad.\n\n\n\n**Designing Responsive Layout**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a clean, responsive, landscape-first design. The goal is the entire grid and tray inside a single responsive `<canvas>`. I\'m focusing on the layout in wide landscape and compact/portrait modes, and ensuring seamless drag-and-drop between the tray and grid. The focus remains on tablet and desktop first, but will elegantly adapt for smaller viewports.\n\n\n**Prioritizing UI Accessibility**\n\nI\'m now integrating D-pad/keyboard accessibility for all elements. I\'m ensuring HUD buttons are reachable via keyboard and D-pad. Thinking about how the Fire TV D-pad and tablet touch users will have a top-tier experience.\n\n\n"
