  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Project Architecture**\n\nI\'m structuring the project in `/src/` for clean, modular code. The goal is to keep core game logic pure, testable, and deterministic. We\'re using React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS v4, Lucide Icons, Vite, and npm scripts for linting and building. I\'m focusing on an architecture that facilitates rigorous unit testing and maintainability.\n\n\n**Outlining Game Data Structures**\n\nI\'m solidifying the data structures within `/src/game/types.ts`. I\'ve defined `GRID_SIZE`, `GameStatus`, `CellOffset`, `BlockPiece`, and the `TileAnimState` with Canvas VFX types (`Particle`, etc.). Added `FocusZone` for navigation and `GameStats`. I\'m now structuring the piece catalog with smart bag in `engine.ts`.\n\n\n**Structuring Piece Catalogs**\n\nI\'m now refining the piece catalog. I\'ve detailed block shapes with sizes from single cells to 4-cell tetrominoes, including various Dominoes, L-trominoes, T-tetrominoes, and a 2x2 square. I\'m focusing on \"Value Pattern Assignment\", and setting up a dynamic base tier pool. I\'m assigning values to cells based on a tiered progression and pattern, ensuring no self-merging on empty boards by careful value selection.\n\n\n**Refining Move Validation**\n\nI\'m now implementing logic to ensure every deal is playable. I\'m focusing on \"Guaranteed Playable Deal\" functionality, swapping larger pieces for smaller ones when necessary. I\'m building out `canPlacePiece` and `findAllValidPlacements` to validate piece placement with clear bounds and emptiness checks. I am creating `hasAnyValidMove` which will look through the tray to ensure at least one move can be made.\nI\'m now structuring a resolution with animation steps for Canvas Engine clarity.\n\n\n**Analyzing Merge Cascades**\n\nI\'m now diving into the cascading merge logic, ensuring grid state determinism. I\'m focusing on tracing a move, starting with placing the piece\'s cells and calculating the base score. Then, the cascading loop begins: I\'ll use BFS or DFS to find and merge clusters of cells with matching values, prioritizing a clear upgrade-path and scoring. I\'m carefully tracking the new cell\'s value and the combo depth for accurate scoring.\n\n\n**Implementing Line Clear Logic**\n\nI\'m now detailing clear logic for full rows and columns, including how to handle simultaneous clears. I\'m focusing on calculating `lineSum` and `lineMultiplier` based on the values of the cells and the number of lines cleared. I\'m recording each `line clear step` for animation. The core logic now correctly clears full rows and columns after each move, which I will test thoroughly.\n\n\n**Finalizing Game State**\n\nI\'m finalizing the game state management now. I\'m removing placed pieces from the tray and dealing new ones if needed, ensuring a playable state. I\'m checking for game over conditions and updating the best score in local storage. All local storage interaction is wrapped in a try/catch. I\'m moving on to building the audio system.\n\nI\'m lazy-initializing the AudioContext and handle muting and unmuting sounds via local storage. I\'m working with synthesizer-based arcade sounds for a cohesive, pure Web Audio API experience. I am creating sounds like `playSelect()`, `playMove()`, `playPlace()`, and `playInvalid()` with a focus on their unique characteristics.\n\n\n**Composing Sound Design**\n\nI\'m now implementing the core sound events. I\'m focusing on the Web Audio API with synthesizer-based sounds, as I create sounds like `playMerge(comboDepth, newValue)` with escalating harmonic neon arpeggios, with higher combo depths shifting up a pentatonic scale. I\'ve designed a `playLineClear(lineCount)` sound for sweeping plasma laser whooshes, and `playNewTray()` for a soft crystalline shimmer. I have also designed sounds for `playGameOver()` and `playNewBest()`.\n\n\\n\\n\n\n**Designing the Game Palette**\n\nI\'m working on `/src/game/palette.ts` to implement a dark obsidian and glowing neon palette. It features a deep dark background with subtle radial vignette, and structural dark slate/indigo grid frame. The tile colors will range across glowing cyan, electric blue, cobalt, violet, neon purple, magenta-indigo, and plasma white-cyan for values from 2 through 4096+.\n\n\n**Defining Game Rendering**\n\nI\'m now detailing the responsive game renderer in `/src/game/renderer.ts`. I\'m calculating the geometry, handling orientation changes with `computeLayout()`, and focusing on a consistent UI across different screen sizes. I\'ve defined a dark obsidian and glowing neon palette in `palette.ts` and specified how the tile colors will increase in value. I am rendering tiles with dark-glass gradient fill and a neon rim stroke.\n\n\n**Refining Renderer Geometry**\n\nI\'m now focusing on calculating the renderer\'s geometry. I\'m ensuring a clean UI across all screen sizes and orientations, handling layout based on aspect ratio with meticulous calculations. I am ensuring that the layout looks correct on devices with a resolution of 1280x800 and up. I am focusing on maintaining a design with a 6x6 grid on either side of a 3-slot tray.\n\n\n**Visualizing Placement Feedback**\n\nI\'m now detailing visual feedback. Valid placements get glowing cyan preview tiles, dashed neon outlines, merge prediction highlights (pulsing rings on merging tiles!), and line clear prediction highlights (glowing edge rails). Invalid placements get crimson outlines. I\'m also implementing squash and pop animations for tile placement, merge slide and fusion animations, line clear beams, and screen shake.\n\n\n**Implementing Game Controls**\n\nI\'m now implementing game controls. I\'m focusing on keyboard and D-pad support for navigation.  I\'m adding animated focus rings to the grid and tray when navigating via keyboard or D-pad. I\'m also implementing the full-viewport Game Shell layout, removing scrollbars and website elements. I am adding a responsive layout to the shell.\n\n\n**Designing Game User Flow**\n\nI\'m now detailing the user flow within the game. I\'ve designed a layout with key features such as a bottom keyboard/D-pad hint in the sidebar, a full stage with a `<canvas>` container, and a layout that adapts to portrait or narrow screen sizes. I\'m focusing on features of the title screen overlay, including the neon design, animated board, and control hints. I\'m also planning the pause modal with essential options.\n\n\n**Finalizing Modal Design**\n\nI\'m now implementing the modals for game over and how-to-play. I am focusing on the \"How to Play\" modal with clear, concise, illustrated controls for touch, mouse, keyboard, and D-pad. The Game Over modal details final and best scores, highest tile, and total stats with \"Play Again\" and \"Main Menu\" options.\n\n\n"
