  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Navigation Patterns**\n\nI\'ve been examining the interplay of navigation history and the `Escape` key\'s functionality, especially concerning `\'H\'`. I realize that a clean design using a Menu History Stack (`viewHistory: GameStatus[]`) will make view transitions better. I\'m focusing on the elegant management of various menu states for maximum user experience.\n\n\n**Mapping Escape Behavior**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the deterministic behavior of the `Escape` key across all possible views and transitions. My focus is a comprehensive mapping of every `GameStatus` state: `\'TITLE_MENU\'`, `\'DAILY_SCREEN\'`, `\'DASHBOARD\'`, `\'PLAYING\'`, `\'PAUSED\'`, and `\'GAME_OVER\'`, including the global `showHelp` overlay. It\342\200\231s critical to ensure that even complex scenarios like the `claimCelebration` modal within the `DashboardScreen` respond predictably and intuitively to `Escape`, preventing unexpected view changes.\n\n\n**Refining Help Overlay Logic**\n\nI\'m currently focused on how the \'How to Play\' overlay interacts with the existing states. I think it is important that the overlay renders on top of the current screen without unmounting. The idea is to keep all underlying views mounted with `aria-hidden` when help is active. When active, I think the keyboard interaction with the canvas should pause until Help is closed. Pressing \'h\', \'H\', \'?\', \'Escape\', \'GoBack\', or \'Backspace\' should close the overlay, play an audio cue, and cleanly return to the prior view, restoring focus where needed.\n\n\n**Tracing Escape Keypaths**\n\nI\'m now charting out every single behavior for the `Escape` key, using a concrete history stack. First, closing the \"How to Play\" overlay. Next, I\'m making sure `Escape` closes the dashboard celebration dialog before navigating the history stack. I\'m also ensuring the key navigates multi-step menus correctly, such as from the daily screen back to the title screen. This includes navigating from the playing state.\n\n\n**Clarifying View Transitions**\n\nI\'m now focusing on transitions from the `TITLE_MENU`. Pressing Escape from the `DAILY_SCREEN` goes to `DASHBOARD`, then `TITLE_MENU`. Also I\'m checking the navigation through `PLAYING`, then `PAUSED` states. The key flow is returning to a `\'PAUSED\'` state before resuming play. This is to ensure safe play at all times.\n\n\n**Centralizing Escape Handling**\n\nI\'m now implementing centralized Escape key handling within `App.tsx` for consistent behavior, even when the canvas doesn\'t have focus. This ensures pressing `Escape` during gameplay always reliably pauses the game and moves to `PAUSED`. I\'ve looked into line 625 and 1143 as part of this process. This approach is key to reliable and intuitive transitions, regardless of focus location.\n\n\n**Defining Game Over Escape**\n\nI\'m now determining the correct behavior for the Escape key within the `GAME_OVER` state. Currently, pressing Escape in `GAME_OVER` has no effect. I am considering different scenarios based on the game mode, either `DAILY_SCREEN` or `TITLE_MENU`, and the path taken to `GAME_OVER`. Specifically, if I arrive from the daily screen, escape should return to the daily screen. Otherwise, escape should return to the title menu. I am working on how to cleanly implement this logic.\n\n\n**Finalizing Escape Flow**\n\nI\'ve finalized the `Escape` key\'s behavior in `GAME_OVER`: from `GAME_OVER`, `Escape` now correctly returns to either the `DAILY_SCREEN` or `TITLE_MENU`, based on the game mode. I also remembered that `TITLE_MENU` should be a no-op with `Escape`. Now, I\'m working on a pure, testable navigation helper module to ensure the history stack avoids cycles or broken states, guaranteeing clean view transitions.\n\n\n**Clarifying Stack Navigation**\n\nI\'m now refining the history stack\'s behavior for intuitive navigation, especially when transitioning through `PLAYING` and `PAUSED` states. I\'ve realized that the stack should contain `[\'TITLE_MENU\', \'DAILY_SCREEN\', \'PLAYING\']`, followed by `PAUSED`, because `PAUSED` is an overlay of `PLAYING`. Now, pressing Escape from `PAUSED` will correctly return to `PLAYING`. I\'m verifying this process through several test cases.\n\n\n**Validating Stack Behavior**\n\nI\'m now carefully validating the history stack\'s behavior across transitions, focusing on the interactions between `DAILY_SCREEN`, `PLAYING`, `PAUSED`, and `GAME_OVER` states. I\'m confirming that the history stack correctly reflects each step, especially the nuances of overlay states like `PAUSED`, ensuring the expected navigation patterns, specifically regarding `GAME_OVER`. Pressing escape correctly returns to `DAILY_SCREEN` when coming from `GAME_OVER`. I\'m also ensuring pressing escape on the daily screen returns to the title menu.\n\n\n**Verifying Stack Integrity**\n\nI\'m now investigating edge cases to strengthen the navigation stack. I\'ve found an issue if pressing `Escape` on the dashboard after being in the playing state and then opening the dashboard from the hud. The stack becomes incorrectly set to `\'TITLE_MENU\', \'PLAYING\', \'PAUSED\'`. The fix is ensuring that going from \'DASHBOARD\' to `\'PAUSED\'` pops the correct view and lands back on the safe `\'PAUSED\'` state. Pressing escape resumes `\'PLAYING\'`.\n\n\n**Optimizing Stack Management**\n\nI\'m now refining the history stack to prevent undesirable behavior like infinite loops. I\'m focusing on scenarios where the same view, such as `DAILY_SCREEN`, gets pushed repeatedly. My approach is to either cap the stack\'s length, dedupe consecutive entries, or truncate to existing occurrences. I\'m considering the expected user experience, especially regarding returning to a previous menu state via `Escape`.\n\n\n**Refining Restart Logic**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the behavior of \'Restart\' from \'PAUSED\' or \'GAME_OVER\'. The restart should eliminate \'PLAYING\', \'PAUSED\', or \'GAME_OVER\' from the history stack, returning to \'TITLE_MENU\' or \'DAILY_SCREEN\'. I\'m verifying this with classic and daily game flows to ensure clean state transitions. This should prevent unexpected history stack outcomes and ensure intuitive game restarts.\n\n\n"
