    thinking: "**Integrating the components**\n\nI\'m incredibly pleased with the clarity of `DashboardScreen.tsx`. My current focus is cleanly integrating everything into `/src/App.tsx`. I\'m working through the imports, specifically `AudioSettingsPanel`, `NeonFlame`, `getFlameTierLabel`, and other functions and components from `audio.ts`, along with `computeCurrentStreak`. The goal is a highly organized, modular setup.\n\n\n**Incorporating New Features**\n\nI\'m now integrating new features, focusing on streak management and audio settings. I\'ve integrated `loadStreakState`, `recordQualifyingStreakDay`, and related functions from `streaks.ts`. I\'m also initializing the `audioSettings` state with `loadAudioSettings` and configuring audio immediately. I\'m carefully synchronizing `recordQualifyingStreakDay` in relevant places. Modal states are being carefully managed for audio settings and streak celebrations.\n\n\n**Refining Reward Logic**\n\nI\'m refining the reward logic. I\'ve ensured `recordQualifyingStreakDay` handles combined Daily Challenge and Daily Reward claims on the same day correctly, preventing double-increments and celebrations. I\'ve also incorporated audio and visual elements for new record celebrations, including neon audio and fireworks. The integration focuses on accurate streak recording and celebratory feedback.\n\n\n**Implementing Audio Controls**\n\nI\'m currently focused on implementing audio controls with refined user experience in mind. The volume slider now correctly clamps, unmutes on drag above zero, updates the neon audio configuration, and saves settings, while the preview functionality has been refined. I\'ve implemented a dedicated `toggleMute()` function that preserves the SFX volume setting. Furthermore, I\'ve incorporated the audio settings panel directly into the pause menu. The next step is adding a modal for comprehensive audio settings, accessible from the HUD.\n\n\n**Adding Audio Panel Access**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing streamlined access to audio settings while preserving existing controls. The key decision involves adding an explicit \"Audio\" button, distinct from the mute toggle. It\'ll be accessible everywhere: the Sidebar, Title Menu, Dashboard top bar, and the Pause Menu. I\'m embedding the full `<AudioSettingsPanel />` in the Pause Menu. Zero existing button behavior changes and comprehensive audio configuration with a single tap is the goal. I\'m also adding a dedicated Streak quick entry point for easy access.\n\n\n"
